---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const fermiCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Decompose the question', note: 'How many piano tuners in Chicago? Break it: pianos per person → tunings per piano per year → tunings per tuner per year. Multiply the chain. Each factor is guessable to within a factor of 3.' },
    { math: '2 · Bound each factor', note: 'Do not guess single numbers — guess ranges ("pianos per 100 people: 1–5"). Errors in a chain multiply, but factors-of-2 up and down cancel statistically: the answer lands within ~10× almost always.' },
    { math: '3 · Sanity-check against knowns', note: 'Compare to anything you know (population, area, speed limits, prices). The check catches unit errors — the most dangerous class of engineering mistake (Mars Climate Orbiter, 1999: lost to newton-pounds confusion).' },
    { math: '4 · Dimensional analysis as a free check', note: 'Units must balance on both sides of every equation — checking them costs seconds and catches most algebra errors. The technique also DERIVES results: scaling laws fall out of pure unit bookkeeping.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/intermediate/units-estimates-and-back-of-envelope">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Back-of-envelope estimation is <strong>the engineer\'s first superpower</strong>: getting
    within an order of magnitude in minutes, before any detailed work — because it tells you
    whether the idea is possible, absurd, or worth designing. Fermi estimated the Trinity
    test\'s yield from shredded paper; the habit is the discipline.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="decompose, bound, sanity-check, check units" widget="equationStep" config={fermiCfg} />

  <h2>Classic Fermi problems as training</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why estimates beat no answer:</strong> an order-of-magnitude answer sorts options now ("is the battery feasible at 10 kg?" — the EV lesson\'s energy density arithmetic); the detailed design can refine later, but only after the gate opens.</li>
    <li><strong>Scaling laws are estimates grown up:</strong> how does X scale with size? Square–cube law (the mensuration lesson): strength ~L², weight ~L³ — one line of dimensional reasoning explains why giant ants are physically impossible and why elephants have thick legs.</li>
    <li><strong>Unit systems cause real failures:</strong> Gimli Glider (1983, fuel in pounds vs kilograms — a 767 glided to a racetrack), Mars Climate Orbiter (1999). Every serious engineering organisation units-checks everything, twice.</li>
    <li><strong>The professional habit:</strong> estimate before calculating, estimate before trusting any number anyone shows you (the data lesson\'s sanity-check, at industrial voltage).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Weinstein & Adam, Guesstimation; Mahajan, Street-Fighting Mathematics; the NASA Mars Climate Orbiter failure report (1999).</p>
</TopicPage>
